WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.340
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:25.360
Oh, Angela.

9
00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:26.860
Hi there.

10
00:00:27.100 --> 00:00:28.080
Hi, everyone.

11
00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:29.660
Hello, hello.

12
00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:32.220
Is everybody ready for a garden party?

13
00:00:33.140 --> 00:00:36.960
Well, I am wearing my Connecticut casual outfit, so.

14
00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:37.280
Good.

15
00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:38.480
Good.

16
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:44.380
This is Season 8, Episode 4, written by Justin Spitzer and directed by Dave Rogers.

17
00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.500
Here is your summary.

18
00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:51.880
Andy throws a dignified garden party at Shroot Farms under the guise of impressing Robert California.

19
00:00:52.480 --> 00:01:00.380
But it soon becomes clear that he is really trying to impress his parents, who clearly favor his younger brother, Walter Jr.

20
00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:06.680
Meanwhile, Pam and Angela discover they have chosen the same name for their unborn sons.

21
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.400
And Jim plays a very elaborate prank on Dwight.

22
00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:12.040
Very elaborate.

23
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:13.160
We'll get into it.

24
00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:15.080
But I was like, seriously, Jim?

25
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.180
I have so many things to say about it and so much mail to read.

26
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:21.940
I was going to say, I hope there was a fan mail flurry.

27
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:22.880
There certainly was.

28
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:33.160
Well, listen, to kick things off for this episode, we have an interview with the one, the only, Walter Jr., Josh Groban.

29
00:01:33.160 --> 00:01:34.760
What a wonderful person.

30
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:36.920
We had the best time talking to him.

31
00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:38.620
We can't wait for you to hear this interview.

32
00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.740
I was on a high all day after we did our interview.

33
00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:43.220
Smiling all day.

34
00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:48.520
Now, listen, Josh Groban, Grammy-nominated singer-songwriter.

35
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:57.460
In 2003, he won two Billboard Music Awards for Classical Crossover Artist of the Year and Classical Crossover Album of the Year for Closer.

36
00:01:57.460 --> 00:02:01.900
His first four solo albums all went multi-platinum.

37
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:06.940
And in 2007, he was the number one best-selling artist in the United States.

38
00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:13.560
And as of 2022, he has sold over 25 million records worldwide.

39
00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:14.900
Wow.

40
00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:16.980
I mean, seriously.

41
00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:23.380
In 2007, he starred in Natasha Pierre and the Great Comet of 1812 on Broadway.

42
00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.420
He was nominated for a Tony Award for Best Actor in a Leading Role in a Musical.

43
00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.540
And he is now currently starring in Sweeney Todd on Broadway.

44
00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:37.880
It has been nominated for eight Tony Awards, including Best Actor in a Musical for Josh Groban.

45
00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:41.140
It is running right now through January 2024.

46
00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:44.500
I am going to get to see this in just a few weeks.

47
00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:45.900
I cannot wait.

48
00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.060
I know.

49
00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:48.600
I wish I was with you.

50
00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:49.340
I'm so bummed.

51
00:02:49.400 --> 00:02:50.660
You're going to have to tell us all about it.

52
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.420
I absolutely will.

53
00:02:52.420 --> 00:02:57.760
Well, I guess without further ado, here is our interview with Josh Groban.

54
00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.550
Oh, my gosh, Josh.

55
00:03:01.790 --> 00:03:03.230
Hello, Josh.

56
00:03:03.490 --> 00:03:04.530
This is so exciting.

57
00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:07.310
Angela and Jenna, I'm so excited to be with you.

58
00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.710
It's been a really long time since I've seen either of you.

59
00:03:10.850 --> 00:03:15.070
Angela and I did a kind of a web thing, a really fun comedy thing.

60
00:03:15.210 --> 00:03:15.670
A web series.

61
00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:20.150
Not too long after we filmed our episode, but it's been a really, really long time.

62
00:03:20.150 --> 00:03:26.010
And Jenna, thank you so much for reaching out and for asking me to be part of this.

63
00:03:26.210 --> 00:03:26.470
Yes.

64
00:03:26.590 --> 00:03:29.470
I slid into your DMs on Instagram.

65
00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:29.950
You did.

66
00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:33.230
And I was so happy you got back to me.

67
00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.830
And I mean, I blew up Angela's phone.

68
00:03:35.990 --> 00:03:37.090
I was like, he wrote back.

69
00:03:37.130 --> 00:03:37.430
She did.

70
00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:37.970
He wrote back.

71
00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:38.710
He said yes.

72
00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:39.530
He said yes.

73
00:03:39.530 --> 00:03:40.570
I saw it.

74
00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:41.370
I'm so thrilled.

75
00:03:41.470 --> 00:03:45.030
And that was very similar to how I wound up being on The Office in general.

76
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:46.170
It was a DM slide.

77
00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:48.430
OK, we wanted to ask you about this.

78
00:03:48.530 --> 00:03:49.970
You have to share with everyone.

79
00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.210
We always ask everyone, how did you get your part on The Office?

80
00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:54.550
So you have to tell the story.

81
00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:58.030
Well, I mean, I can imagine that I was very similar to

82
00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:03.170
many people who were asked to be on the show and that we were all just enormous fans of the show.

83
00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:11.030
And so to get a request to say, hey, this might be a fun thing for you to do was just like finding the golden ticket.

84
00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:11.990
It was an amazing thing.

85
00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:15.890
So Mindy Kaling did the DM slide on the old Twitter.

86
00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:16.390
Remember Twitter?

87
00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:16.870
Yep.

88
00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:17.930
Oh, yeah.

89
00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:19.290
Those were the days.

90
00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:28.230
And so Mindy sent me a message saying, hey, we're writing an episode and we think there might be a role that would be really fun for you.

91
00:04:28.450 --> 00:04:30.170
Are you in the L.A.

92
00:04:30.650 --> 00:04:32.190
area during this date?

93
00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.850
And I was on tour at that time.

94
00:04:34.970 --> 00:04:37.710
I was actually doing a really, really big tour at that time.

95
00:04:37.830 --> 00:04:42.430
And so I really didn't think when she mentioned it that I would have any time at all.

96
00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:45.950
And it was going to absolutely crush my soul to not be able to do it.

97
00:04:46.250 --> 00:04:48.890
But it landed right when I was playing L.A.

98
00:04:49.090 --> 00:04:58.450
And so during my time off between shows, I was able to take a little road trip up to Shroot FarMs.

99
00:04:58.450 --> 00:05:04.490
And it was the best recharge I could have possibly imagined.

100
00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:05.250
It was so fun.

101
00:05:05.390 --> 00:05:07.390
It was I mean, I talk about it even to this day.

102
00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:13.170
It was really one of the one of my most favorite days, you know, that I've ever had in the business or out of the business.

103
00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:13.990
It was just the beSt.

104
00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:14.790
Oh, my gosh.

105
00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:16.110
So what made it so fun?

106
00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:17.990
What what do you love about it?

107
00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:26.650
Well, I mean, having done now some TV since then, I can tell you that shooting like at a lovely outdoor estate is a delight.

108
00:05:26.650 --> 00:05:34.090
Like to not be to not be at a soundstage, to not have to spend 15 hours in a little, you know,

109
00:05:34.230 --> 00:05:43.390
box of a room where you're not seeing daylight and to be able to shoot a on a beautiful kind of setting.

110
00:05:43.390 --> 00:05:52.070
But then also, again, as a fan of the show to sometimes when you make a guest appearance on a show, you're doing it with one person or two,

111
00:05:52.170 --> 00:05:57.350
two people from the show and you're part of like a little arc that doesn't involve other people in the caSt.

112
00:05:57.350 --> 00:06:13.210
And so to be part of a everybody together doing a party scene meant that as a fanboy of the show, I got to sit and just be around like the entire cast for the entirety of the day.

113
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:20.930
And so it was just, you know, I felt like I'd I felt like I'd kind of like won an auction or something that like, you know,

114
00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.350
come come hang at Shrewd Farms with the cast of The Office.

115
00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:25.490
It was the beSt.

116
00:06:25.490 --> 00:06:29.490
And of course, because you're sitting around a lot during shooting of something,

117
00:06:29.610 --> 00:06:36.370
there's just a lot of time to just get to know everybody and get to know the writers and and the wonderful crew and and just chat with everybody.

118
00:06:36.370 --> 00:06:37.870
And it was just such a dream.

119
00:06:38.090 --> 00:06:42.050
I loved location episodes just for that reason, too.

120
00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:43.690
There was a lot of hanging out.

121
00:06:43.850 --> 00:06:47.550
I have a picture of us just all casually on the porch at Shrewd FarMs.

122
00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:47.830
Yeah.

123
00:06:48.030 --> 00:06:48.830
And we're just chatting.

124
00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:51.850
And we were all so charmed by you, Josh.

125
00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:53.010
We were so excited.

126
00:06:53.010 --> 00:06:55.410
And I know I was trying to play it cool.

127
00:06:55.590 --> 00:06:57.850
I'm sure Jenna, too, because we're two of the biggest dorks ever.

128
00:06:58.030 --> 00:07:01.850
But you were instantly you were instantly Andy's brother.

129
00:07:02.050 --> 00:07:03.090
It was crazy.

130
00:07:03.310 --> 00:07:04.290
It was so perfect.

131
00:07:05.350 --> 00:07:09.970
I loved that they wrote, you know, sometimes I play like myself.

132
00:07:10.290 --> 00:07:10.410
Yeah.

133
00:07:10.510 --> 00:07:11.330
Like Parks and Rec.

134
00:07:11.350 --> 00:07:12.290
You played yourself.

135
00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:13.850
Like in Parks and Rec.

136
00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:13.990
Yeah.

137
00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:15.010
I played myself.

138
00:07:15.290 --> 00:07:16.570
And Glee, I played myself.

139
00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:23.930
And I'm just one of those people like because my music side is like so serious, like whenever somebody wants me to play a bizarro version of me,

140
00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:27.610
it just like lets me kind of let that other side play around a little bit.

141
00:07:27.610 --> 00:07:32.450
And so I say yes to playing myself weirdly sometimes.

142
00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:38.870
This was an opportunity like this was really, really fun to, you know, to play Andy's brother.

143
00:07:39.010 --> 00:07:49.130
And when Mindy, Mindy and BJ kind of laid out for me, you know, what the story is, the jealousy, the sibling rivalry and the jealousy and and why, you know, I got to be Walter Jr.

144
00:07:49.130 --> 00:07:51.950
And they passed over, you know, Ed.

145
00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:52.950
Yeah, the namesake.

146
00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:53.590
Yeah, exactly.

147
00:07:53.990 --> 00:07:57.990
It just every bit of it was so funny to me.

148
00:07:58.530 --> 00:08:01.170
My brother and I have the same birthday four years apart.

149
00:08:01.730 --> 00:08:10.550
So we have had we've we had our own like little sibling, like we've never had any rivalry, but he's now like my best friend and he's like the beSt.

150
00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:18.450
But like when you get like when you have to share a birthday, you know, there's that there's that weird sibling tension when it's like four and eight, you know, and.

151
00:08:19.130 --> 00:08:19.530
Yeah.

152
00:08:19.590 --> 00:08:22.950
How do you do that party for both a four year old and an eight year old?

153
00:08:23.650 --> 00:08:24.050
Yeah.

154
00:08:24.050 --> 00:08:33.530
We discovered very early on that all, you know, because parties are organized by the parents at that age, the kids were not we're not in charge of presents that we're just kids.

155
00:08:33.790 --> 00:08:43.730
So we realized very early on that the parents, if they knew that it was also big brother Josh's birthday, they would bring a little extra present.

156
00:08:43.970 --> 00:08:50.790
You know, we got we got to bring an extra present because it's also so we had we we figured out early on that same birthday meant double the presents.

157
00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:55.110
And so so we we mean we had a little deal.

158
00:08:55.430 --> 00:08:56.250
We made it work.

159
00:08:56.390 --> 00:08:57.030
You made it work.

160
00:08:57.290 --> 00:08:58.050
Yes, we made it work.

161
00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:05.150
One of the things I loved in rewatching this episode was that Andy's brother seems like he genuinely just loves Andy.

162
00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:08.890
He's his Bronard and he was so excited to meet Tuna.

163
00:09:09.390 --> 00:09:10.010
Yes, exactly.

164
00:09:10.050 --> 00:09:12.110
He clearly looks up to his brother.

165
00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:13.670
And I thought that was really nice.

166
00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:21.070
It's like whatever Andy's feeling, I don't I didn't feel like his brother was trying to stir the pot, you know, totally.

167
00:09:21.350 --> 00:09:25.370
I think that a lot of it is, you know, is is within Andy.

168
00:09:25.530 --> 00:09:29.790
There's there's a lot of there's a lot of stuff that Andy's kind of like, you know, needs therapy about.

169
00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:32.390
But it's it's and that's that can happen.

170
00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:34.910
And maybe at some point there was a little bit of that rub.

171
00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:37.550
But I think that now it's like everybody's grown up.

172
00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:41.750
And I think that truly I felt one of the things I loved about Walter Jr.

173
00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:46.530
Is that like he's like this super talented golden retriever that just like doesn't know.

174
00:09:47.070 --> 00:09:49.030
He's like, oh, yeah, sure.

175
00:09:49.050 --> 00:09:53.930
I'll go fetch that acoustic guitar and, you know, sing this sing this beautiful angelic song for all of you.

176
00:09:54.370 --> 00:09:58.330
And Andy is just eating him up inside, you know, and yeah,

177
00:09:58.570 --> 00:10:05.730
I think that that kind of lack of awareness was one of the things that really made that dynamic really adorable and really fun.

178
00:10:05.950 --> 00:10:10.730
And, you know, I think I think the parents knew better a little bit, but I don't think Walter Jr.

179
00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:11.190
knew better.

180
00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:11.910
I think Walter Jr.

181
00:10:12.030 --> 00:10:14.350
is just just, you know, living his dream.

182
00:10:14.390 --> 00:10:20.270
And it's like there's always those people out there that just have that little extra serotonin that you're just like, how that must be nice.

183
00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:27.710
How do you, how do you, God, if I could live in that brain for just one day, you know, that would be really, really fun.

184
00:10:27.850 --> 00:10:29.710
And I think that, you know, Walter Jr.

185
00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.510
is one of those people where it's just like he keeps falling up.

186
00:10:32.710 --> 00:10:32.930
Yeah.

187
00:10:33.250 --> 00:10:36.550
And Andy's just had to be kind of next to that, you know, at all times.

188
00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:41.670
And a side note to that, which is really funny because now I'm doing Sweeney Todd is that, you know,

189
00:10:41.730 --> 00:10:45.030
one of my other very favorite episodes of The Office is the Sweeney Todd episode.

190
00:10:45.030 --> 00:10:52.850
And it's one that we all kind of quote and and love very much backstage at our Sweeney Todd is that, of course, Andy, you know, is in Sweeney Todd.

191
00:10:53.110 --> 00:10:53.290
Yes.

192
00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:54.810
And is Antony in Sweeney Todd.

193
00:10:55.050 --> 00:10:58.310
And now you're in Sweeney Todd, but you're the lead.

194
00:10:58.630 --> 00:10:59.810
And you're on Broadway.

195
00:11:00.030 --> 00:11:00.270
Yes.

196
00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.370
So you have bested him again.

197
00:11:03.090 --> 00:11:05.050
I have bested him yet again.

198
00:11:05.090 --> 00:11:09.150
And that is the universe being so weird and so wonderful.

199
00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:12.050
And so, yes, yeah, it's it's great.

200
00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:17.170
But and I'm I'm hoping at some point Ed and I can really kind of sit down and have a proper talk and hug it out.

201
00:11:18.950 --> 00:11:19.950
That would be awesome.

202
00:11:20.230 --> 00:11:23.210
When you were on set, Josh, did you ever break in a scene?

203
00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:24.170
Oh, yeah.

204
00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:24.870
All the time.

205
00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:26.250
Oh, yeah, absolutely.

206
00:11:26.770 --> 00:11:32.590
I guess one of my questions for you, because I was only I did two episodes and spent a day doing each episode.

207
00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:38.910
But I kind of wanted to ask you guys as well, you know, was that was there always room for improv on your days?

208
00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:42.450
Because because the scripts were brilliant.

209
00:11:42.710 --> 00:11:47.250
Each one, every time I read it, it was so conversational and it felt so natural.

210
00:11:47.250 --> 00:11:54.290
And there were oftentimes things that were written that if I hadn't known it was written, I would think, oh, that must have been an improvised line.

211
00:11:54.810 --> 00:11:56.770
And that's just that's just how wonderful it was.

212
00:11:56.770 --> 00:12:00.730
But there was such wonderful improv happening at Shroot FarMs.

213
00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:06.230
Everybody was just kind of pitching things and and even off camera, just so everybody's just so funny.

214
00:12:06.330 --> 00:12:18.170
Kate Flannery was always finding new ways to like get me, you know, and with like the idea of like pulling my vocal cords out of my throat, you know, that kind of thing.

215
00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:28.230
And so, yeah, I mean, was that always encouraged or was that something that kind of developed over time or or was that just the writing made it seem that way at all times?

216
00:12:28.390 --> 00:12:29.950
It was sort of all of those things.

217
00:12:29.950 --> 00:12:35.670
I mean, it did always start with an amazing script, but it was always a living, breathing thing.

218
00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:43.690
So the writers of that episode and the producers would come down and they were always pitching alternate lines.

219
00:12:43.910 --> 00:12:44.090
Pitching.

220
00:12:44.390 --> 00:12:44.690
Yeah.

221
00:12:44.930 --> 00:12:49.750
And they would throw them out to the actors or the actors might have an idea in the moment.

222
00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:51.190
And it was always allowed.

223
00:12:51.630 --> 00:12:51.810
Yeah.

224
00:12:51.930 --> 00:12:55.850
You know, as long as we had gotten it in a scripted way, at least once.

225
00:12:56.330 --> 00:12:56.430
Yeah.

226
00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:57.670
Then we could play around.

227
00:12:58.150 --> 00:12:58.510
Right.

228
00:12:58.870 --> 00:12:59.790
That's really cool.

229
00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:05.630
And also just being kind of like an outsider in the group, I had not done a lot of acting at that time.

230
00:13:05.750 --> 00:13:07.730
And I'd certainly not done a lot of comedy acting at that time.

231
00:13:07.850 --> 00:13:15.270
